WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:05.340
&gt;&gt; We're super excited

00:00:05.341 --> 00:00:08.440
today to have Ann Cudd joining
us from Boston University.

00:00:08.441 --> 00:00:10.130
Ann is the Dean of Arts and

00:00:10.131 --> 00:00:12.270
Sciences at Boston University.

00:00:12.271 --> 00:00:14.465
She's also a professor
of philosophy.

00:00:14.466 --> 00:00:16.950
Before that, she was at
the University of Kansas,

00:00:16.951 --> 00:00:20.685
where I was leaving
behind me in the alley.

00:00:20.686 --> 00:00:23.270
I said she was not
living in the alley.

00:00:23.271 --> 00:00:27.095
She was living across
the alley in a lovely home,

00:00:27.096 --> 00:00:29.000
a lovely home, yes.

00:00:29.001 --> 00:00:32.960
I've raised her since she
was a whippersnapper.

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:39.720
Ann is a very interesting person
for this lab because she

00:00:39.721 --> 00:00:42.150
has a undergraduate degree

00:00:42.151 --> 00:00:44.610
in mathematics,
she's a philosopher,

00:00:44.611 --> 00:00:47.080
she wrote her dissertation
on game theory,

00:00:47.081 --> 00:00:50.410
she studies feminism,
she studies oppression,

00:00:50.411 --> 00:00:52.350
and she has a lot of
overlaps with a lot

00:00:52.351 --> 00:00:54.645
of different interests
that a lot of us have,

00:00:54.646 --> 00:00:58.375
so I thought it would be
delightful to have her come by.

00:00:58.376 --> 00:01:00.955
I will let you go ahead.

00:01:00.956 --> 00:01:03.355
She has tons of
accomplishments, lots of books,

00:01:03.356 --> 00:01:04.925
lots of praise,
all of that stuff,

00:01:04.926 --> 00:01:07.425
but I would rather hear
what you have to say.

00:01:07.426 --> 00:01:09.905
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much, Nancy.

00:01:09.906 --> 00:01:14.525
It's such a privilege to come
here and to speak with you,

00:01:14.526 --> 00:01:18.420
and thank you for allowing
a philosopher to come in

00:01:18.421 --> 00:01:23.880
and talk about the metaphysics
of the cell parts.

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:26.760
I don't think that's what Nancy

00:01:26.761 --> 00:01:30.065
had initially imagined
that I would speak about,

00:01:30.066 --> 00:01:31.915
but I told her this is actually

00:01:31.916 --> 00:01:34.980
the most recent research paper
that I've been working on,

00:01:34.981 --> 00:01:37.015
so she said," Well, if
you're passionate about it,

00:01:37.016 --> 00:01:38.720
then go for it."

00:01:38.721 --> 00:01:43.190
So, I am promising
some implications of

00:01:43.191 --> 00:01:45.150
my work for something that

00:01:45.151 --> 00:01:47.925
I think maybe many of
you are interested in.

00:01:47.926 --> 00:01:50.905
So basically let me
just get started,

00:01:50.906 --> 00:01:55.920
and let me provide some context
of why I'm thinking about

00:01:55.921 --> 00:01:58.600
self-ownership and
how this could play

00:01:58.601 --> 00:02:00.550
a role in those kinds of

00:02:00.551 --> 00:02:02.715
things that many of
you think about.

00:02:02.716 --> 00:02:05.010
So, if you think about it,

00:02:05.011 --> 00:02:08.110
there's a very basic moral
or political question

00:02:08.111 --> 00:02:09.740
that moral and political
philosophers

00:02:09.741 --> 00:02:11.045
should try to answer,

00:02:11.046 --> 00:02:12.860
and that is something like what

00:02:12.861 --> 00:02:15.300
two persons have a
right to do and say,

00:02:15.301 --> 00:02:18.480
and what can society or

00:02:18.481 --> 00:02:20.770
other individuals force us to

00:02:20.771 --> 00:02:23.480
do on pain of some
social sanction.

00:02:23.481 --> 00:02:26.944
Whether that's from
just disapproval,

00:02:26.945 --> 00:02:30.015
all the way to
incarceration or worse.

00:02:30.016 --> 00:02:33.180
Okay, so any kind
of social sanction.

00:02:33.181 --> 00:02:35.125
So what do we have a right to do

00:02:35.126 --> 00:02:38.580
and what can society
force us to do?

00:02:38.581 --> 00:02:41.615
Basically, philosophers' method,

00:02:41.616 --> 00:02:44.470
the typical philosophers' method

00:02:44.471 --> 00:02:48.000
is to establish
some basic concepts,

00:02:48.001 --> 00:02:51.225
some basic principles,
go to first principles,

00:02:51.226 --> 00:02:52.925
that can then ground

00:02:52.926 --> 00:02:55.620
a theory that can
answer the questions.

00:02:55.621 --> 00:03:01.500
Okay, so our theory is
usually built up with

00:03:01.501 --> 00:03:04.220
some kind of argument from

00:03:04.221 --> 00:03:08.675
example or analogy
or counterexample,

00:03:08.676 --> 00:03:11.685
and really it's pretty basic,

00:03:11.686 --> 00:03:14.165
and so it's not
usually empirical,

00:03:14.166 --> 00:03:16.495
although as we were talking
about at lunch today,

00:03:16.496 --> 00:03:18.450
sometimes philosophers

00:03:18.451 --> 00:03:21.155
do a little bit of
experimental work,

00:03:21.156 --> 00:03:24.930
but I'm afraid I'm not one
of those philosophers.

00:03:24.931 --> 00:03:28.409
So, it's basic argumentation,

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:30.615
so that's the kind of
methodology I'm going to use.

00:03:30.616 --> 00:03:34.745
So self-ownership is one
of these basic concepts.

00:03:34.746 --> 00:03:38.415
In particular, it's a basic
concept for libertarianism.

00:03:38.416 --> 00:03:40.320
So libertarianism,
I'm going to be

00:03:40.321 --> 00:03:42.570
speaking of libertarianism,
not very much,

00:03:42.571 --> 00:03:44.320
but a little bit today as

00:03:44.321 --> 00:03:46.300
a political philosophy and not

00:03:46.301 --> 00:03:49.420
necessarily in
the sort of Rand Paul,

00:03:49.421 --> 00:03:53.315
contemporary political

00:03:53.316 --> 00:03:57.680
empirically practically
political world, okay.

00:03:57.681 --> 00:03:59.125
I'm going to be talking about.

00:03:59.126 --> 00:04:01.505
Insofar as I do talk
about libertarianism,

00:04:01.506 --> 00:04:02.500
I'm going to be talking about

00:04:02.501 --> 00:04:04.370
the basic political philosophy.

00:04:04.371 --> 00:04:09.390
So, what libertarians do with

00:04:09.391 --> 00:04:15.740
self-ownership as they say
that they posit this idea,

00:04:15.741 --> 00:04:18.810
and they don't usually
examine it very carefully,

00:04:18.811 --> 00:04:23.490
but they posit the idea that
each of us owns ourselves,

00:04:23.491 --> 00:04:29.555
and they especially
concentrate on this idea of

00:04:29.556 --> 00:04:32.020
ownership and that brings a lot

00:04:32.021 --> 00:04:36.660
of moral political
weight with it.

00:04:36.661 --> 00:04:39.955
It's because ownership
rights are taken to be

00:04:39.956 --> 00:04:42.950
very stranger rights.
The rights to

00:04:42.951 --> 00:04:47.460
use or possess or exclude
others from using,

00:04:47.461 --> 00:04:51.090
rights to dispose of or
transfer some kind of a thing.

00:04:51.091 --> 00:04:53.820
So if I own my toothbrush,

00:04:53.821 --> 00:04:56.235
I can use it, I possess it,

00:04:56.236 --> 00:04:59.010
I can exclude you from using it,

00:04:59.011 --> 00:05:03.200
I hope, I can throw it
away when I want to,

00:05:03.201 --> 00:05:05.355
and if I want to give it
away, I can give it away.

00:05:05.356 --> 00:05:07.140
So there's something about,

00:05:07.141 --> 00:05:12.605
we think about ownership as
having those kinds of claims.

00:05:12.606 --> 00:05:15.514
Now of course, you
can take those apart,

00:05:15.515 --> 00:05:20.600
you can make up
different kinds of ownership

00:05:20.601 --> 00:05:23.180
rights legally, you can take
all of those pieces apart,

00:05:23.181 --> 00:05:27.730
but basically, this
is why libertarians

00:05:27.731 --> 00:05:30.290
are interested in this idea
of self-ownership is

00:05:30.291 --> 00:05:31.560
because it gives us a very

00:05:31.561 --> 00:05:34.880
solid significant stringent claim

00:05:34.881 --> 00:05:36.605
over our own bodies,

00:05:36.606 --> 00:05:38.615
and then by extension,

00:05:38.616 --> 00:05:41.530
over the things
that our bodies do,

00:05:41.531 --> 00:05:43.540
and in particular, the ways that

00:05:43.541 --> 00:05:46.535
we gain the right to property.

00:05:46.536 --> 00:05:49.550
So libertarians, well, if you

00:05:49.551 --> 00:05:53.050
think about even old liberals,

00:05:53.051 --> 00:05:55.490
classical liberals
like John Locke,

00:05:55.491 --> 00:05:59.450
who argued that from
the ownership of ourselves,

00:05:59.451 --> 00:06:02.465
when we work on
some unknowns nature and

00:06:02.466 --> 00:06:06.355
we we pluck an apple from
an apple tree that nobody owns,

00:06:06.356 --> 00:06:09.565
we come to own that
through our labor.

00:06:09.566 --> 00:06:13.405
We come to own the apple
that we have now labored on,

00:06:13.406 --> 00:06:15.435
by an extension of
our own bodies.

00:06:15.436 --> 00:06:18.630
Anyway, I'm not really going
to talk much about that,

00:06:18.631 --> 00:06:20.449
I'm just trying to motivate

00:06:20.450 --> 00:06:25.335
the idea that
self-ownership provides,

00:06:25.336 --> 00:06:27.100
especially for the libertarian,

00:06:27.101 --> 00:06:29.635
this important stringent right

00:06:29.636 --> 00:06:32.945
over our bodies and
then over our things.

00:06:32.946 --> 00:06:36.835
Libertarians appeal
to this idea to say,

00:06:36.836 --> 00:06:39.080
first of all, that
for each of us,

00:06:39.081 --> 00:06:41.865
our bodies or ourselves,

00:06:41.866 --> 00:06:45.055
whatever a self is in
addition to a body,

00:06:45.056 --> 00:06:47.070
but over our bodies,

00:06:47.071 --> 00:06:51.450
we cannot be used or possessed
without our consent.

00:06:51.451 --> 00:06:54.225
That's pretty important
right, right?

00:06:54.226 --> 00:06:59.630
That's one thing, and another
is that our duties to

00:06:59.631 --> 00:07:02.115
help others or to do
things for others

00:07:02.116 --> 00:07:05.775
can only arise from
our voluntary consent.

00:07:05.776 --> 00:07:08.540
So Libertarians are very keen to

00:07:08.541 --> 00:07:13.260
say that you cannot
force me to help

00:07:13.261 --> 00:07:16.730
you unless I consent to some kind

00:07:16.731 --> 00:07:20.430
of contractual arrangement
with you, okay.

00:07:20.431 --> 00:07:22.865
So that's how you
get this idea of

00:07:22.866 --> 00:07:26.854
libertarianism that's
against taxation,

00:07:26.855 --> 00:07:29.555
that's for very stringent
property rights,

00:07:29.556 --> 00:07:35.200
and builds this cocoon,

00:07:35.201 --> 00:07:37.565
if you will, around
the individual,

00:07:37.566 --> 00:07:41.735
and I'm characterizing a little
bit so if our character,

00:07:41.736 --> 00:07:43.460
so if you're a libertarian,

00:07:43.461 --> 00:07:47.429
please feel free to
attack that caricature,

00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:49.100
but I'm trying to do it for

00:07:49.101 --> 00:07:51.385
the sake of simplicity
and clarity,

00:07:51.386 --> 00:07:54.405
and it's overly
simplistic, perhaps.

00:07:54.406 --> 00:07:57.310
Okay, so that's the idea of

00:07:57.311 --> 00:08:00.845
self-ownership as the
libertarian looks at it.

00:08:00.846 --> 00:08:03.670
The ideal itself, I think,

00:08:03.671 --> 00:08:06.175
has two very important
characteristics.

00:08:06.176 --> 00:08:08.200
It's one very the
individualistic.

00:08:08.201 --> 00:08:10.380
So the self is to be fully

00:08:10.381 --> 00:08:12.730
identified with the individual.

00:08:12.731 --> 00:08:17.150
On some theories, that's
the individual body,

00:08:17.950 --> 00:08:22.350
and on other theories,

00:08:22.351 --> 00:08:24.000
well you don't have to just

00:08:24.001 --> 00:08:26.395
identify the self with the body,

00:08:26.396 --> 00:08:29.770
it could be with the rational
individual that's inside of

00:08:29.771 --> 00:08:33.680
me or a rational individual.

00:08:33.681 --> 00:08:37.125
The thing that is the agent

00:08:37.126 --> 00:08:38.660
in game theory or something

00:08:38.661 --> 00:08:41.280
like that or in decision theory,

00:08:41.470 --> 00:08:44.280
and the others' aspect of it

00:08:44.281 --> 00:08:46.480
is that it's an atomistic theory.

00:08:46.481 --> 00:08:49.790
So individuals are separable

00:08:49.791 --> 00:08:53.505
from the external world,
at least in principle.

00:08:53.506 --> 00:08:56.080
So the people who

00:08:56.081 --> 00:09:02.025
populate a libertarian theory
are these self-contained,

00:09:02.026 --> 00:09:07.665
self-sufficient rational
individuals, okay.

00:09:07.666 --> 00:09:10.770
So that might be familiar to some

00:09:10.771 --> 00:09:13.485
of you economists,
and the theory,

00:09:13.486 --> 00:09:16.025
basically the theory
of homo economicus

00:09:16.026 --> 00:09:17.365
or something like that,

00:09:17.366 --> 00:09:20.305
it's not not that
different from that.

00:09:20.306 --> 00:09:23.490
So what I'm going to do
in my argument today is,

00:09:23.491 --> 00:09:25.380
I'm going to deny both
of these aspects of

00:09:25.381 --> 00:09:28.770
self-ownership and argue
that a self owner,

00:09:28.771 --> 00:09:32.405
so an entity that can own itself

00:09:32.406 --> 00:09:35.000
and that can be

00:09:35.001 --> 00:09:38.325
the foundation of
a political or moral theory,

00:09:38.326 --> 00:09:42.595
is actually necessarily
connected to other self owners.

00:09:42.596 --> 00:09:44.505
So is not atomistic,

00:09:44.506 --> 00:09:47.550
but connected within a network

00:09:47.551 --> 00:09:49.750
of norms and social practices,

00:09:49.751 --> 00:09:52.195
and he social practices of

00:09:52.196 --> 00:09:55.725
guiding each other
according to social norms.

00:09:55.726 --> 00:10:00.100
So that guidance and being
guided by and guiding

00:10:00.101 --> 00:10:04.850
others according to
norms, rules, practices,

00:10:04.851 --> 00:10:06.575
things that judgments of

00:10:06.576 --> 00:10:09.385
better and worse,
that kind of thing,

00:10:09.386 --> 00:10:11.860
are really really
important to making us

00:10:11.861 --> 00:10:14.940
the kind of things we are namely,

00:10:14.941 --> 00:10:17.460
self owners that can be

00:10:17.461 --> 00:10:21.420
the foundation of a moral and
political philosophy.

00:10:21.421 --> 00:10:23.910
So are you with me?

00:10:23.911 --> 00:10:27.875
That's the basic context here
that we're talking about.

00:10:27.876 --> 00:10:31.885
Okay, so I want to
draw sharp disarray,

00:10:31.886 --> 00:10:34.170
a careful distinction between

00:10:34.171 --> 00:10:37.600
descriptive and
normative individualism.

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:42.575
So, I take myself
to be a liberal,

00:10:42.576 --> 00:10:46.080
in the large, broad
sense of liberal.

00:10:46.081 --> 00:10:47.890
In the sense of what I would

00:10:47.891 --> 00:10:50.065
say is normative individualism.

00:10:50.066 --> 00:10:53.289
That is, I take the human self,

00:10:53.290 --> 00:10:57.805
whatever it is, to be of
ultimate intrinsic moral value.

00:10:57.806 --> 00:11:00.495
So, the whole point, to me,

00:11:00.496 --> 00:11:04.865
of doing political and
moral philosophy, is to,

00:11:04.866 --> 00:11:08.790
in some sense, give

00:11:08.791 --> 00:11:13.615
a theory that protects
that individual moral self.

00:11:13.616 --> 00:11:16.770
We are of ultimate value.

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:22.835
Descriptive individualism
by contrast

00:11:22.836 --> 00:11:27.560
is just claims that the human
self can be described and

00:11:27.561 --> 00:11:29.870
explained by reference to

00:11:29.871 --> 00:11:31.760
forces and causes
that act only on

00:11:31.761 --> 00:11:34.865
the individual and not

00:11:34.866 --> 00:11:39.435
having to refer to
any irreducibly social facts.

00:11:39.436 --> 00:11:43.575
So, I think that descriptive
individualism is false,

00:11:43.576 --> 00:11:46.285
but normative
individualism is true.

00:11:46.286 --> 00:11:50.370
I think that
the atomistic self-owner

00:11:50.371 --> 00:11:58.275
of libertarianism is a
descriptive, individualist theory.

00:11:58.276 --> 00:12:01.145
It's also normatively,
individualist.

00:12:01.146 --> 00:12:03.930
But I deny the descriptive part,

00:12:03.931 --> 00:12:06.820
I don't deny the normative part.

00:12:07.160 --> 00:12:09.750
So, as I say, liberalism

00:12:09.751 --> 00:12:12.195
rests on normative individualism,

00:12:12.196 --> 00:12:15.510
but it need not embrace
a descriptive one.

00:12:15.511 --> 00:12:19.920
I think that libertarianism
is a form of liberalism that

00:12:19.921 --> 00:12:24.720
embraces both descriptive
and normative individualism.

00:12:24.721 --> 00:12:26.820
Just to give you a contrast,

00:12:26.821 --> 00:12:28.800
and I'm not going to be talking
about this view at all,

00:12:28.801 --> 00:12:34.625
but communitarianism denies
normative individualism

00:12:34.626 --> 00:12:37.985
as well as descriptive
individualism.

00:12:37.986 --> 00:12:39.650
Okay? Yes.

00:12:39.651 --> 00:12:44.745
&gt;&gt; Is it necessarily either
or when you think about it,

00:12:44.746 --> 00:12:50.020
is it possible to both be
a normative individualist

00:12:50.021 --> 00:12:53.940
and also communitarianism that

00:12:53.941 --> 00:12:56.010
says each self is of ultimate
in terms of moral value.

00:12:56.011 --> 00:12:57.970
So, two is a
community in which we

00:12:57.971 --> 00:13:02.030
always I before another?

00:13:02.031 --> 00:13:04.240
&gt;&gt; I don't think that's
impossible and that's my view,

00:13:04.241 --> 00:13:06.055
but I think that's a liberalism.

00:13:06.056 --> 00:13:08.090
But that's a really
good question.

00:13:08.091 --> 00:13:14.450
So, here's where I think there's

00:13:14.451 --> 00:13:17.840
the two might- I would
have to hear more about

00:13:17.841 --> 00:13:19.460
what you mean by
communitarianism but

00:13:19.461 --> 00:13:21.765
where they might split apart.

00:13:21.766 --> 00:13:22.655
&gt;&gt; It's all there.

00:13:22.656 --> 00:13:25.055
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well, where they
might split apart is

00:13:25.056 --> 00:13:28.490
suppose you think that we

00:13:28.491 --> 00:13:32.570
should sacrifice individuals for

00:13:32.571 --> 00:13:35.935
the sake of preservation
of some community.

00:13:35.936 --> 00:13:38.480
So, for instance, not allow

00:13:38.481 --> 00:13:41.900
a right of exit from a community.

00:13:41.901 --> 00:13:44.610
There are communities that

00:13:45.820 --> 00:13:51.090
don't allow individuals
to exit without very,

00:13:51.091 --> 00:13:54.725
very significant cause that

00:13:54.726 --> 00:13:58.880
I would say is a denial
of right of exit.

00:13:58.881 --> 00:14:01.820
To me, that's anti-liberal.

00:14:04.760 --> 00:14:07.500
But then you might
say, "Well, okay,

00:14:07.501 --> 00:14:10.035
but then that's denying
normative individualism."

00:14:10.036 --> 00:14:13.500
So, I think you

00:14:13.501 --> 00:14:16.735
can't be a normative,
individualist and communitarian.

00:14:16.736 --> 00:14:25.425
So, here's the thesis
that I'm going to defend.

00:14:25.426 --> 00:14:28.950
I'm going to argue that
self-owners can't be

00:14:28.951 --> 00:14:31.200
the atomistic
individuals portrayed

00:14:31.201 --> 00:14:33.585
by the concept of
libertarian self-ownership.

00:14:33.586 --> 00:14:36.180
They're essentially
constituted by

00:14:36.181 --> 00:14:38.130
their connections
with other selfs.

00:14:38.131 --> 00:14:40.040
So, this word "Essentially"

00:14:40.041 --> 00:14:43.115
is obviously a pretty important
word for a philosopher.

00:14:43.116 --> 00:14:46.445
It means I'm going to start
talking metaphysics here,

00:14:46.446 --> 00:14:48.500
because it's got to be really,

00:14:48.501 --> 00:14:50.620
that's a strong claim,

00:14:50.621 --> 00:14:52.710
essential is really basic.

00:14:52.711 --> 00:14:54.290
So, I'm giving a metaphysical

00:14:54.291 --> 00:14:55.760
arguments because I think that's

00:14:55.761 --> 00:14:58.925
a more stringent argument
than a merely causal one.

00:14:58.926 --> 00:15:03.135
So, lots of libertarians will
say to me, "Oh, of course,

00:15:03.136 --> 00:15:06.050
we think that infants
have to be cared for by

00:15:06.051 --> 00:15:09.380
their parents and people
need all kinds of care,

00:15:09.381 --> 00:15:10.670
and so causally yes,

00:15:10.671 --> 00:15:12.130
we're all dependent
on each other.

00:15:12.131 --> 00:15:18.860
Sure." But essentially, we
are separable individuals.

00:15:18.861 --> 00:15:20.750
So, that's why I
think I have to go

00:15:20.751 --> 00:15:22.985
to this metaphysical claim,

00:15:22.986 --> 00:15:25.125
or metaphysical plane
if you will.

00:15:25.126 --> 00:15:27.760
So, join me in this quest,

00:15:27.761 --> 00:15:30.650
on this metaphysical plane,

00:15:30.980 --> 00:15:34.455
to really make the argument.

00:15:34.456 --> 00:15:37.040
In the end, if

00:15:37.041 --> 00:15:39.450
you all want to say or
somebody you want to say,

00:15:39.451 --> 00:15:41.860
"Well, really why do we care
about the metaphysics?"

00:15:41.861 --> 00:15:43.520
In some sense I'll say, "Yeah,

00:15:43.521 --> 00:15:45.530
I understand where
you're coming from,

00:15:45.531 --> 00:15:47.300
because what's really
important ultimately is

00:15:47.301 --> 00:15:49.560
the politics and what

00:15:49.561 --> 00:15:51.945
we actually hold each
other accountable for."

00:15:51.946 --> 00:15:55.290
But my view is that if you
want to defeat the argument,

00:15:55.291 --> 00:15:58.290
the best rational argument
of the libertarian,

00:15:58.291 --> 00:16:01.375
you have to go to
this metaphysical plane. Yes.

00:16:01.376 --> 00:16:03.210
&gt;&gt; Are you predicted on
the idea of there being

00:16:03.211 --> 00:16:04.740
a singular self,

00:16:04.741 --> 00:16:07.410
fixed singular self,
as opposed to

00:16:07.411 --> 00:16:12.000
a dynamic or multiplicity
of selves in a person?

00:16:12.001 --> 00:16:17.220
&gt;&gt; I don't think it
is dependent on that.

00:16:17.221 --> 00:16:23.340
However, if there's not
a connection among those cells,

00:16:23.341 --> 00:16:25.250
then it's hard to say what

00:16:25.251 --> 00:16:29.130
the right self would have
if it changes over time.

00:16:29.131 --> 00:16:31.620
So, I think there has
to be some continuity.

00:16:31.621 --> 00:16:33.990
So, I would subscribe to

00:16:33.991 --> 00:16:36.240
a psychological continuity thesis

00:16:36.241 --> 00:16:37.720
about personal identity.

00:16:37.721 --> 00:16:41.790
If that's basically
the philosophical problem

00:16:41.791 --> 00:16:44.400
I think that you're referring to.

00:16:44.401 --> 00:16:47.205
But within that, of course,

00:16:47.206 --> 00:16:50.010
psychological continuity
is consistent

00:16:50.011 --> 00:16:52.305
with a lot of change over time

00:16:52.306 --> 00:16:54.720
just as long as there's a memory

00:16:54.721 --> 00:16:59.210
or a connection from
one moment to the next.

00:17:05.390 --> 00:17:10.080
So, here's just to say
a little bit more about why

00:17:10.081 --> 00:17:12.015
this has to be
metaphysical argument

00:17:12.016 --> 00:17:14.520
and not a causal one,

00:17:14.521 --> 00:17:17.040
or to say a little bit more
about what that means.

00:17:17.041 --> 00:17:20.040
So, I think you can
separate out what I

00:17:20.041 --> 00:17:23.375
would call the causal origins
of any given self.

00:17:23.376 --> 00:17:26.745
So, I was born on a certain day,

00:17:26.746 --> 00:17:29.445
to a certain pair of parents,

00:17:29.446 --> 00:17:35.310
with a certain set of
genetic components,

00:17:35.311 --> 00:17:40.080
nurtured by the food
that they gave

00:17:40.081 --> 00:17:46.680
me and then taught by the words
that they spoke to me.

00:17:46.681 --> 00:17:53.535
The various stimulants
that they presented to me.

00:17:53.536 --> 00:17:55.245
Then over time, as I

00:17:55.246 --> 00:17:57.750
became a child and

00:17:57.751 --> 00:17:59.670
started playing with
peers and so forth,

00:17:59.671 --> 00:18:02.700
there were further
causal interactions

00:18:02.701 --> 00:18:04.320
that have formed me,

00:18:04.321 --> 00:18:06.930
up to the point where I
am the person that I am.

00:18:06.931 --> 00:18:13.690
So, those are all
causal origins of this self.

00:18:15.920 --> 00:18:19.845
The metaphysical
origins of a self,

00:18:19.846 --> 00:18:23.350
though are something
a little bit different,

00:18:23.570 --> 00:18:26.010
this is the question, what are

00:18:26.011 --> 00:18:27.960
the necessary conditions for

00:18:27.961 --> 00:18:32.985
something to be a self
that is a self-owner?

00:18:32.986 --> 00:18:35.460
So, that's a very much more basic

00:18:35.461 --> 00:18:37.650
and conceptual question than

00:18:37.651 --> 00:18:39.690
the causal question of how

00:18:39.691 --> 00:18:42.000
did I get to be the self
that I am today?

00:18:42.001 --> 00:18:46.860
Or even the generic causal
question of how do

00:18:46.861 --> 00:18:48.720
people generally grow up to

00:18:48.721 --> 00:18:51.540
be the adult human beings
that they are?

00:18:51.541 --> 00:18:53.670
So, you can ask
that causal question

00:18:53.671 --> 00:18:54.900
in those two separate ways.

00:18:54.901 --> 00:18:57.570
A very particular way
and a more generic way

00:18:57.571 --> 00:19:01.140
that has to do with
the causal laws.

00:19:01.141 --> 00:19:03.450
By causal I mean

00:19:03.451 --> 00:19:10.415
not just physical but also
mental and social causes.

00:19:10.416 --> 00:19:12.910
So, the things that
I think right now,

00:19:12.911 --> 00:19:15.210
the self having these thoughts

00:19:15.211 --> 00:19:18.600
about metaphysics and so forth.

00:19:18.601 --> 00:19:24.975
Right now as I speak, has
social causes and mental causes.

00:19:24.976 --> 00:19:30.580
I've had previous thoughts
about philosophy,

00:19:30.581 --> 00:19:33.025
about, whatever. All
of those things.

00:19:33.026 --> 00:19:34.950
That have made me

00:19:34.951 --> 00:19:37.535
think the things that
I think right now.

00:19:37.536 --> 00:19:41.375
I guess I am
deterministic, right?

00:19:41.376 --> 00:19:45.060
That's just to say
the self is deterministic,

00:19:45.061 --> 00:19:48.520
and their social causes.

00:19:48.521 --> 00:19:51.520
There are all kinds of
different sciences that would

00:19:51.521 --> 00:19:54.680
play a nice role in
explaining how human beings

00:19:54.681 --> 00:19:57.920
come to be the things
that they are today in

00:19:57.921 --> 00:20:02.860
this culture with these
thoughts, with these tendencies.

00:20:03.110 --> 00:20:06.000
But metaphysical
origins has to do

00:20:06.001 --> 00:20:08.194
with what is a self-owner?

00:20:08.195 --> 00:20:11.345
How does it come to be
the thing that it is?

00:20:11.346 --> 00:20:15.100
If you're thinking about
moral and political philosophy,

00:20:15.101 --> 00:20:16.900
and what you wanna do is,

00:20:16.901 --> 00:20:20.620
have a theory that
holds regardless

00:20:20.621 --> 00:20:23.540
of the particularities of

00:20:23.541 --> 00:20:27.235
how the being came into
existence causally.

00:20:27.236 --> 00:20:29.570
And philosophers tend
to want to do that,

00:20:29.571 --> 00:20:32.600
because we want to have a
moral and political philosophy

00:20:32.601 --> 00:20:34.464
that would work for robots.

00:20:34.465 --> 00:20:36.200
Or for entities on

00:20:36.201 --> 00:20:39.810
a distant exoplanet,
right? Doesn't matter.

00:20:39.811 --> 00:20:41.490
Provided that they are

00:20:41.491 --> 00:20:43.640
the kinds of things
that are self-owners,

00:20:43.641 --> 00:20:47.350
this moral and political theory
should fit for them.

00:20:48.280 --> 00:20:54.590
Okay? Okay. So, the assumptions

00:20:54.591 --> 00:20:57.750
I'm making about
what a self-owner

00:20:57.751 --> 00:21:01.635
is or has to be in order to be

00:21:01.636 --> 00:21:05.725
the grounding for social and
political philosophy

00:21:05.726 --> 00:21:08.330
or for moral and
political philosophy is that,

00:21:08.331 --> 00:21:12.240
those self-owners have to
be intrinsically valuable.

00:21:12.241 --> 00:21:15.045
They have to be capable of making

00:21:15.046 --> 00:21:21.745
and respecting ownership claims.

00:21:21.746 --> 00:21:24.185
In other words, what
is it to be an owner?

00:21:24.186 --> 00:21:27.840
Well, it's to be able to
make a claim on something.

00:21:27.841 --> 00:21:29.995
So, that's the basic assumption.

00:21:29.996 --> 00:21:35.955
What is it to be
normative individualism to hold?

00:21:35.956 --> 00:21:37.430
Well, has to be intrinsically

00:21:37.431 --> 00:21:39.070
valuable and not valuable just

00:21:39.071 --> 00:21:42.650
because of the advantages
it brings to somebody,

00:21:42.651 --> 00:21:44.420
it's something else, right?

00:21:44.421 --> 00:21:46.735
Has to be valuable in itself.

00:21:46.736 --> 00:21:50.200
And the part about respecting
ownership claims-.

00:21:50.201 --> 00:21:53.670
And there, I think it's
going to be an entity that's

00:21:53.671 --> 00:21:57.470
a grounding for a moral and
political philosophy,

00:21:57.471 --> 00:22:02.350
then it has to be capable of
basically acting morally.

00:22:02.351 --> 00:22:05.230
So, it has to be able
to respect the claims

00:22:05.231 --> 00:22:08.280
of others. Just capable of that.

00:22:08.281 --> 00:22:10.535
Might turn out to be
an immoral version

00:22:10.536 --> 00:22:12.020
of one of these beings
but at least it's

00:22:12.021 --> 00:22:16.315
capable of understanding
and respecting them.

00:22:16.316 --> 00:22:17.850
So, as I say though,

00:22:17.851 --> 00:22:19.590
the metaphysical question doesn't

00:22:19.591 --> 00:22:21.410
assume that self-owners are

00:22:21.411 --> 00:22:23.060
necessarily human or even

00:22:23.061 --> 00:22:25.895
biological or that
they're embodied.

00:22:25.896 --> 00:22:27.980
And an analogy to

00:22:27.981 --> 00:22:31.160
this kind of metaphysical
question I'm asking is-.

00:22:31.161 --> 00:22:35.035
Think about chess
pieces and chessboards.

00:22:35.036 --> 00:22:37.490
You could have a set of

00:22:37.491 --> 00:22:40.410
chess pieces that are

00:22:40.411 --> 00:22:42.885
made out of ivory and you
could have a wooden board.

00:22:42.886 --> 00:22:47.465
Or you could have
a digital representation of

00:22:47.466 --> 00:22:50.240
chess pieces and the rules

00:22:50.241 --> 00:22:52.940
of chess and chessboards, right?

00:22:52.941 --> 00:22:54.860
And both of them are equally

00:22:54.861 --> 00:22:56.860
chess pieces and chessboards.

00:22:56.861 --> 00:22:58.275
It doesn't matter.

00:22:58.276 --> 00:23:01.635
So, what makes them that
is the set of rules

00:23:01.636 --> 00:23:05.090
that you're applying to

00:23:05.091 --> 00:23:07.325
the movements of
the chess pieces.

00:23:07.326 --> 00:23:10.149
Okay? Yes.

00:23:10.150 --> 00:23:14.000
&gt;&gt; Are there examples you
can give that would satisfy

00:23:22.130 --> 00:23:23.160
the criteria that would

00:23:23.161 --> 00:23:23.191
not be human biological
or embodied?

00:23:23.191 --> 00:23:23.251
&gt;&gt; Well not human. Might be
some other social primates.

00:23:23.251 --> 00:23:23.256
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:23:23.256 --> 00:23:23.321
&gt;&gt; That be easy. But
I'm not sure of that,

00:23:23.321 --> 00:23:23.341
because I'm not
a biological anthropologist.

00:23:23.341 --> 00:23:23.366
But I bet maybe
baboons are like this.

00:23:23.366 --> 00:23:23.401
I don't know, they could
respect. Biological? Well, you

00:23:23.401 --> 00:23:23.451
could imagine a virtual beings.

00:23:23.451 --> 00:23:23.481
In fact, that would count
as not embodied too, right?

00:23:23.481 --> 00:23:24.520
Algorithms there might be Hal?

00:23:59.260 --> 00:24:01.840
Right? It could be a self-owner.

00:24:01.841 --> 00:24:05.485
I don't think that Hal could
be a connected self-owner.

00:24:05.486 --> 00:24:06.760
No. Maybe Hal is connected to

00:24:06.761 --> 00:24:09.235
the people that he lived among.

00:24:09.236 --> 00:24:10.645
&gt;&gt; Dave?

00:24:10.646 --> 00:24:13.170
&gt;&gt; Dave. Right.
Maybe between them.

00:24:13.171 --> 00:24:17.460
Yes. But that's
where I'm trying to

00:24:17.461 --> 00:24:22.235
leave open the possibility
of a virtual being,

00:24:22.236 --> 00:24:24.400
and the idea that there

00:24:24.401 --> 00:24:27.110
might be an artificial
intelligence where you would say,

00:24:27.111 --> 00:24:30.600
it would be immoral to unplug it

00:24:30.601 --> 00:24:34.080
or to kill it because it's
intrinsically valuable.

00:24:34.081 --> 00:24:35.740
Why? Because it's capable of

00:24:35.741 --> 00:24:40.045
the same capabilities of
self-ownership that we are.

00:24:40.046 --> 00:24:42.000
I don't think it exists yet,

00:24:42.001 --> 00:24:44.360
but in our dreams, right?

00:24:45.250 --> 00:24:47.570
Okay. Good. So, this is

00:24:47.571 --> 00:24:51.090
just a little representation

00:24:51.091 --> 00:24:53.040
of these sort of hierarchies.

00:24:53.041 --> 00:24:56.870
So, they're actually
existing self-owners,

00:24:56.871 --> 00:25:01.230
are a subset of
the physically possible ones.

00:25:01.231 --> 00:25:03.880
But the metaphysically
possible ones are

00:25:03.881 --> 00:25:07.130
even beyond a bigger set, right?

00:25:07.131 --> 00:25:10.955
I think that's just it.
Okay. So, here we go.

00:25:10.956 --> 00:25:15.680
Here's the argument. Okay.
Now I need my notes.

00:25:15.681 --> 00:25:19.595
Okay. So, the first premise
is just to say that,

00:25:19.596 --> 00:25:21.710
if the self-owners are to be

00:25:21.711 --> 00:25:25.245
foundational for moral and
political philosophy,

00:25:25.246 --> 00:25:28.805
then they're going to have to
have intrinsic moral value,

00:25:28.806 --> 00:25:31.410
and they have to have the ability

00:25:31.411 --> 00:25:34.165
to participate some
community or polity.

00:25:34.166 --> 00:25:36.240
It's just from the meaning of

00:25:36.241 --> 00:25:38.910
moral theory or
political philosophy really.

00:25:38.911 --> 00:25:43.550
Okay? If they're
not valuable then,

00:25:43.551 --> 00:25:47.620
then they're not sort of
the subject of a moral theory.

00:25:47.621 --> 00:25:50.435
Okay. If they're valuable-.

00:25:50.436 --> 00:25:51.880
I should draw the distinction

00:25:51.881 --> 00:25:54.595
between intrinsic and
instrumental value.

00:25:54.596 --> 00:25:56.320
I'm assuming that.

00:25:56.321 --> 00:25:58.700
So, something that's
intrinsically valuable

00:25:58.701 --> 00:26:01.060
is valuable in and
of itself, right?

00:26:01.061 --> 00:26:02.510
Without considering
anything else.

00:26:02.511 --> 00:26:03.860
Something that's merely

00:26:03.861 --> 00:26:06.370
instrumentally
valuable is valuable

00:26:06.371 --> 00:26:09.125
because it's valuable
to something

00:26:09.126 --> 00:26:12.190
that is intrinsically
valuable, right?

00:26:12.191 --> 00:26:15.810
So, the subjects of
moral and political theory,

00:26:15.811 --> 00:26:17.160
I believe have to be.

00:26:17.161 --> 00:26:18.645
I don't think this controversial.

00:26:18.646 --> 00:26:21.525
Have to be
intrinsically valuable.

00:26:21.526 --> 00:26:28.109
And what is it to participate
in a community or a polity?

00:26:28.110 --> 00:26:30.330
Well, minimally I think,

00:26:30.331 --> 00:26:35.875
it means that you're able
to reflect on norms,

00:26:35.876 --> 00:26:41.420
rules or something
more informal rules

00:26:41.660 --> 00:26:44.210
that are given to you

00:26:44.211 --> 00:26:47.720
externally and guide your
actions according to them.

00:26:47.721 --> 00:26:54.060
So for instance, to learn
that it's bad or wrong

00:26:54.061 --> 00:27:01.040
to do X and it's good
or right to do Y,

00:27:01.041 --> 00:27:08.240
or just some basic rules and
some basic normative claims,

00:27:08.241 --> 00:27:13.820
something is apt, appropriate,
good, right preferable.

00:27:13.821 --> 00:27:16.659
Any of those kind of judgments.

00:27:16.660 --> 00:27:22.045
Okay? And then even more,

00:27:22.046 --> 00:27:24.720
fully than that,
more fulsomely is

00:27:24.721 --> 00:27:27.400
to be able to guide
others actions.

00:27:27.401 --> 00:27:28.800
Minimally, you have to be able to

00:27:28.801 --> 00:27:30.480
guide your own
actions according to

00:27:30.481 --> 00:27:33.540
these rules, but even better,

00:27:33.541 --> 00:27:36.690
real participation in
a polity or community,

00:27:36.691 --> 00:27:38.160
is that you are not

00:27:38.161 --> 00:27:42.445
just the subject but
you're the agent as well.

00:27:42.446 --> 00:27:48.840
Okay? And that means, I think,

00:27:48.841 --> 00:27:52.440
in order to be able to
do that reflection on

00:27:52.441 --> 00:27:56.320
norms and guiding one's actions
according to those norms,

00:27:56.321 --> 00:27:58.875
one has to have mental states

00:27:58.876 --> 00:28:03.150
and intentions, beliefs, desires.

00:28:03.151 --> 00:28:07.080
Okay? One has to be able
to see what the norm is,

00:28:07.081 --> 00:28:10.920
consider an action, and judge

00:28:10.921 --> 00:28:14.280
whether that action fits
the norm or doesn't.

00:28:14.281 --> 00:28:17.910
And likewise, judge
about others actions.

00:28:17.911 --> 00:28:22.315
Do they fit according
to the norm or not?

00:28:22.316 --> 00:28:24.510
Okay? And right now,

00:28:24.511 --> 00:28:25.740
I'm using norm in

00:28:25.741 --> 00:28:28.895
a very descriptive way
in the sense that,

00:28:28.896 --> 00:28:31.760
it might be a bad norm, right?

00:28:31.761 --> 00:28:36.819
But it's a norm agreed
upon by some community.

00:28:36.820 --> 00:28:38.270
Okay? Some group.

00:28:38.271 --> 00:28:43.790
&gt;&gt; Does it trouble
your definition of technology?

00:28:43.791 --> 00:28:45.960
Actions that violate
norms as participation,

00:28:45.961 --> 00:28:47.290
if I'm murdered in cold blood,

00:28:47.291 --> 00:28:49.390
Am I still participating
in the community

00:28:49.391 --> 00:28:51.560
or have I stopped participating
in the community?

00:28:51.561 --> 00:28:53.420
&gt;&gt; Right. I'm just
trying to get-.

00:28:53.421 --> 00:28:57.010
As long as you can see
that you violated a norm,

00:28:57.011 --> 00:29:00.100
then you're participating
in this very basic sense.

00:29:00.101 --> 00:29:00.790
&gt;&gt; Acknowledgement.

00:29:00.791 --> 00:29:03.410
&gt;&gt; Acknowledgement,
yes, at minimal.

00:29:03.411 --> 00:29:05.860
And I'm just trying to get
the minimum qualifications

00:29:05.861 --> 00:29:07.360
for something to be a self owner,

00:29:07.361 --> 00:29:08.440
again because I'm trying to get

00:29:08.441 --> 00:29:10.945
this metaphysical very broad view

00:29:10.946 --> 00:29:14.110
and include everything
in that we possibly can,

00:29:14.111 --> 00:29:16.670
and the reason for
that is because,

00:29:16.671 --> 00:29:18.730
suppose I make a very broad-.

00:29:18.731 --> 00:29:20.570
All of these things could be

00:29:20.571 --> 00:29:22.880
self owners but
the one thing that can't,

00:29:22.881 --> 00:29:27.015
are the atomistic individuals
of libertarian theory.

00:29:27.016 --> 00:29:31.865
Right? That's the
structure of my argument.

00:29:31.866 --> 00:29:34.370
Okay. So, have to have

00:29:34.371 --> 00:29:36.170
mental states,
intentions, beliefs,

00:29:36.171 --> 00:29:40.070
desires, so that they
can take in norms,

00:29:40.071 --> 00:29:42.500
reflect on norms or rules.

00:29:42.501 --> 00:29:44.780
And what is it to be an owner?

00:29:44.781 --> 00:29:47.180
Well minimally, again, that's to

00:29:47.181 --> 00:29:51.650
have a claim over a
thing that is owned and

00:29:51.651 --> 00:29:54.260
a claim against others over

00:29:54.261 --> 00:29:58.880
others exclusively claiming it.

00:29:58.881 --> 00:30:01.070
And an ability make
use of or possess it.

00:30:01.071 --> 00:30:02.750
Again, this is just from

00:30:02.751 --> 00:30:05.359
the very definition of ownership.

00:30:05.360 --> 00:30:08.160
What it is to own something.

00:30:09.010 --> 00:30:12.455
Okay? So what I am saying though,

00:30:12.456 --> 00:30:17.825
is that one has to be able
to actively make a claim.

00:30:17.826 --> 00:30:20.480
And that again is

00:30:20.481 --> 00:30:23.300
bringing in intentions
and beliefs and desires.

00:30:23.301 --> 00:30:30.180
Okay? So I'm concluding

00:30:30.181 --> 00:30:33.030
from those last two premises
about ownership and

00:30:33.031 --> 00:30:34.560
participation that
a cell phone or

00:30:34.561 --> 00:30:36.690
must be a thing that
intends and acts,

00:30:36.691 --> 00:30:39.085
and that can take and
respond to norms.

00:30:39.086 --> 00:30:43.680
And that's just what in
other words is a moral person.

00:30:43.681 --> 00:30:47.115
So, philosophers always talk
about what is a person,

00:30:47.116 --> 00:30:49.230
and there's a high bar

00:30:49.231 --> 00:30:50.880
for person-hood it's
not just having

00:30:50.881 --> 00:30:52.530
the right number of chromosomes

00:30:52.531 --> 00:30:54.855
or roughly the right number
of chromosomes,

00:30:54.856 --> 00:30:57.570
it's actually having
this ability to

00:30:57.571 --> 00:31:01.545
intend and act and take
and respond to norms.

00:31:01.546 --> 00:31:04.960
So, a self owner has
to be a moral person.

00:31:09.730 --> 00:31:16.420
I guess key premise is six,

00:31:16.421 --> 00:31:18.440
which is to say that,

00:31:18.441 --> 00:31:23.440
what makes a self's existence
as this moral person,

00:31:23.441 --> 00:31:25.510
as this thing that
can think, intend,

00:31:25.511 --> 00:31:28.890
and act, is that it's

00:31:28.891 --> 00:31:32.930
embedded in a normative web
of meaningfulness.

00:31:32.931 --> 00:31:36.210
Why is that? Well, if I'm
going to have an intention,

00:31:36.211 --> 00:31:43.170
I intend to do X or I intend
some meaningful thing,

00:31:43.171 --> 00:31:46.570
it has to have a meaning for me.

00:31:46.571 --> 00:31:49.280
But meaning then immediately,

00:31:49.281 --> 00:31:52.310
I think brings in language.

00:31:52.311 --> 00:31:55.290
And language is
essentially social.

00:31:55.291 --> 00:31:58.410
One individual,
atomistic individual,

00:31:58.411 --> 00:32:00.305
cannot come up with a language.

00:32:00.306 --> 00:32:03.235
A language is essentially social.

00:32:03.236 --> 00:32:05.585
I believe that once we

00:32:05.586 --> 00:32:09.070
start to talk about things
as having intentions,

00:32:09.071 --> 00:32:11.560
beliefs, and desires,
we've already brought in

00:32:11.561 --> 00:32:14.715
the social, namely language.

00:32:14.716 --> 00:32:17.380
But there are lots
of other norms that

00:32:17.381 --> 00:32:19.705
also make our world meaningful.

00:32:19.706 --> 00:32:22.600
So, our judgments are
better and worse,

00:32:22.601 --> 00:32:25.885
apt and inapt, good and bad,

00:32:25.886 --> 00:32:28.710
also color the whole world
that we live in.

00:32:28.711 --> 00:32:36.010
We never see anything as
green their chair, right?

00:32:36.011 --> 00:32:39.715
We see it as, it's
a certain kind of green,

00:32:39.716 --> 00:32:42.140
it's a chair that you can sit in,

00:32:42.141 --> 00:32:44.535
but you wouldn't want
to lie down in it.

00:32:44.536 --> 00:32:47.100
There are all these
judgments that

00:32:47.101 --> 00:32:49.570
immediately come
with our perception.

00:32:49.571 --> 00:32:52.845
We never perceive things
purely as some data,

00:32:52.846 --> 00:32:55.695
but always we perceive
it as something,

00:32:55.696 --> 00:33:01.950
and that I believe brings
in norms and language.

00:33:01.951 --> 00:33:03.270
And if that's the case,

00:33:03.271 --> 00:33:06.285
so that cracks
the argument for me.

00:33:06.286 --> 00:33:08.940
If that's the case,
then we are essentially

00:33:08.941 --> 00:33:12.940
connected in this web
of social norms.

00:33:12.941 --> 00:33:15.675
We are essentially
socially connected.

00:33:15.676 --> 00:33:18.095
Do you want to ask a question?

00:33:18.096 --> 00:33:20.415
&gt;&gt; I was just curious whether

00:33:20.416 --> 00:33:25.490
somebody who understands
some of the norms

00:33:25.491 --> 00:33:28.460
but not all the norms or
respects some of the norms

00:33:28.461 --> 00:33:32.500
not all the norms would be
considered an immoral person,

00:33:32.501 --> 00:33:33.785
because they don't respect
some of the norms?

00:33:33.786 --> 00:33:37.230
&gt;&gt; Yes, I haven't even
gotten to that point.

00:33:37.231 --> 00:33:41.120
I would say jumping
ahead a little bit.

00:33:41.121 --> 00:33:42.810
There are many norms in

00:33:42.811 --> 00:33:44.160
any given community that are

00:33:44.161 --> 00:33:46.680
bad norms and should be flouted.

00:33:46.681 --> 00:33:49.550
There are others that
are optional, right?

00:33:49.551 --> 00:33:55.745
It's normative for women to
paint their fingernails,

00:33:55.746 --> 00:33:59.180
but it's optional, right?

00:33:59.181 --> 00:34:02.015
So there are these things that

00:34:02.016 --> 00:34:04.880
maybe is a general judgments of

00:34:04.881 --> 00:34:08.940
better and worse in a community
that you don't need to.

00:34:08.941 --> 00:34:11.240
&gt;&gt; I've been thinking
about your inability

00:34:11.241 --> 00:34:12.240
to recognize a norm.

00:34:12.241 --> 00:34:13.240
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:34:13.241 --> 00:34:18.515
&gt;&gt; You can recognize some but
you don't recognize others.

00:34:18.516 --> 00:34:21.080
&gt;&gt; I like if you
can recognize one.

00:34:21.081 --> 00:34:22.600
You count as one of

00:34:22.601 --> 00:34:26.750
these intrinsically
valuable, self-owners.

00:34:26.751 --> 00:34:27.920
&gt;&gt; Even if you go against it?

00:34:27.921 --> 00:34:30.095
&gt;&gt; Yes, right. Even
going against it, right?

00:34:30.096 --> 00:34:32.540
&gt;&gt; If you don't
even recognize it?

00:34:32.541 --> 00:34:34.630
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:34:35.010 --> 00:34:38.795
Yes. There is no probably you

00:34:38.796 --> 00:34:46.425
No second person who
would be kind of said-.

00:34:46.426 --> 00:34:48.700
Well, somebody who has
become comatose and will

00:34:48.701 --> 00:34:51.415
never regain consciousness again,

00:34:51.416 --> 00:34:58.610
will count as not a
self owner on this.

00:34:58.611 --> 00:35:00.100
Doesn't mean you can
do whatever you want

00:35:00.101 --> 00:35:02.050
to to anything that's
not a self owner.

00:35:02.051 --> 00:35:03.790
I'm not saying that, therefore,

00:35:03.791 --> 00:35:05.680
we can cut down all the
trees in the world

00:35:05.681 --> 00:35:08.455
because they're not self owners.
I don't wanna say that.

00:35:08.456 --> 00:35:11.350
But I'm simply trying
to make this claim that

00:35:11.351 --> 00:35:13.190
anything that we're
going to ground

00:35:13.191 --> 00:35:15.220
moral or political philosophy on,

00:35:15.221 --> 00:35:16.990
has to be something that can

00:35:16.991 --> 00:35:22.040
recognize and guide itself
according to norms.

00:35:25.180 --> 00:35:28.430
Yes?

00:35:28.431 --> 00:35:31.920
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] I think this is an
example of that kind of thing.

00:35:32.260 --> 00:35:36.710
It feels like the concept
of the self owner,

00:35:36.711 --> 00:35:38.470
you're saying that needs to be

00:35:38.471 --> 00:35:40.250
practical for moral philosophy.

00:35:40.251 --> 00:35:41.390
And therefore, it
has to be grounded

00:35:41.391 --> 00:35:43.085
in notion of participation.

00:35:43.086 --> 00:35:46.025
And then participation has
norms and norms great-

00:35:46.026 --> 00:35:46.371
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:35:46.371 --> 00:35:47.885
&gt;&gt; -connections,
impulse, et cetera.

00:35:47.886 --> 00:35:50.640
So, should I take
away that this is not

00:35:50.641 --> 00:35:52.890
a useful concept
without the communities

00:35:52.891 --> 00:35:55.340
or roughly or I
should think of it-.

00:35:55.341 --> 00:35:57.380
it's not even a well-defined
concept to that conclusion.

00:35:57.381 --> 00:35:58.010
&gt;&gt; Right, yes.

00:35:58.011 --> 00:36:02.365
I think the idea that
a self owner could

00:36:02.366 --> 00:36:06.390
be separable from
all other possible self owners,

00:36:06.391 --> 00:36:08.590
like there could be one in

00:36:08.591 --> 00:36:11.800
the history of
the Big Bang happens,

00:36:11.801 --> 00:36:13.675
then there's one self owner,

00:36:13.676 --> 00:36:15.910
Robinson Crusoe let's call him,

00:36:15.911 --> 00:36:19.795
he's out there and
that's impossible.

00:36:19.796 --> 00:36:22.645
That's metaphysically impossible,

00:36:22.646 --> 00:36:24.490
because he could
never have language,

00:36:24.491 --> 00:36:28.390
he could never have norms to
guide himself according to.

00:36:28.391 --> 00:36:31.390
There's also the fact that well,

00:36:31.391 --> 00:36:32.790
who's going to make
claims against?

00:36:32.791 --> 00:36:35.240
But that's not the point
that I'm making.

00:36:35.241 --> 00:36:38.770
&gt;&gt; Okay.
&gt;&gt; Right? Yes. He might make

00:36:38.771 --> 00:36:40.540
claims against
this flying asteroid

00:36:40.541 --> 00:36:43.520
that's going by or
something. Yes?

00:36:46.230 --> 00:36:49.440
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] You take a human
being and now you take him

00:36:49.441 --> 00:36:51.770
to another planet alone
and he stays there.

00:36:51.771 --> 00:36:54.815
Should he himself think of
himself as a self owner?

00:36:54.816 --> 00:36:57.920
&gt;&gt; Good. Yes. Yes. So, I

00:36:57.921 --> 00:37:01.520
think that once you've
acquired norms,

00:37:01.521 --> 00:37:02.930
and you remember them,

00:37:02.931 --> 00:37:04.800
and you think, crap I miss

00:37:04.801 --> 00:37:07.320
my family but they
would have wanted

00:37:07.321 --> 00:37:09.570
me to go on or that's

00:37:09.571 --> 00:37:12.000
already a normative way
of guiding your actions.

00:37:12.001 --> 00:37:13.770
So, you've internalized
these norms.

00:37:13.771 --> 00:37:15.240
I think we can internalize them,

00:37:15.241 --> 00:37:18.975
and then you know be
all alone in the world.

00:37:18.976 --> 00:37:22.130
Now, if you then get hit
by the asteroid and you

00:37:22.131 --> 00:37:25.610
have amnesia and you remember
no norms and no language,

00:37:25.611 --> 00:37:28.100
you're longer a self owner.

00:37:28.101 --> 00:37:30.725
Yes. So, the Robinson
Crusoe story,

00:37:30.726 --> 00:37:33.860
which is often brought
up by libertarians as

00:37:33.861 --> 00:37:38.060
an example of
the ideal man, right?

00:37:38.061 --> 00:37:41.870
Is to me, it's

00:37:41.871 --> 00:37:46.430
loaded already with all
of the norms that he was,

00:37:46.431 --> 00:37:49.010
so didn't keep a diary and stuff.

00:37:49.011 --> 00:37:53.015
He was very much trying to
live like a proper Englishman,

00:37:53.016 --> 00:37:57.110
that's a very normative
framework to be living within.

00:37:57.111 --> 00:38:03.260
Okay.

00:38:03.261 --> 00:38:06.790
So, this normative web I
think is constitutive of-.

00:38:06.791 --> 00:38:08.590
Philosophers love
to use that term.

00:38:08.591 --> 00:38:11.390
-Constitutive of self-hood.

00:38:11.391 --> 00:38:13.300
And this is just to
reiterate the main points of

00:38:13.301 --> 00:38:15.940
my central argument and
that is to be a person,

00:38:15.941 --> 00:38:20.010
is to be enmeshed in a web
of social relations.

00:38:20.011 --> 00:38:23.050
The normative web that
we are enmeshed in is

00:38:23.051 --> 00:38:25.570
this emergent normative
framework that

00:38:25.571 --> 00:38:28.600
we collectively create through

00:38:28.601 --> 00:38:31.755
our individual behavior
and social interactions.

00:38:31.756 --> 00:38:35.640
Right? But our individual
behavior then creates norms,

00:38:35.641 --> 00:38:42.165
nobody set out to-
Probably to- I don't know.

00:38:42.166 --> 00:38:43.660
Create some silly norm that there

00:38:43.661 --> 00:38:45.385
exists today in
fashion or something

00:38:45.386 --> 00:38:49.390
that somebody tried something

00:38:49.391 --> 00:38:50.785
out and all of
a sudden other people

00:38:50.786 --> 00:38:54.250
started copying it and
pretty soon became

00:38:54.251 --> 00:38:55.720
a normative way of

00:38:55.721 --> 00:38:58.910
dressing or acting or
something like that.

00:38:59.900 --> 00:39:02.770
To use a Heideggerian phrase,

00:39:02.771 --> 00:39:05.350
which I rarely will do but
this is my favorite one.

00:39:05.351 --> 00:39:08.300
Social institutions are
always already there.

00:39:08.301 --> 00:39:11.545
Okay? So we're dropped
into as individuals.

00:39:11.546 --> 00:39:13.120
In fact, were sort
of dropped into

00:39:13.121 --> 00:39:14.970
this normative world
that we find,

00:39:14.971 --> 00:39:18.600
and that shapes us
inevitably. Yes?

00:39:18.601 --> 00:39:21.270
&gt;&gt; Is there any reason why
the web is your metaphor,

00:39:21.271 --> 00:39:31.170
like spider web as [inaudible]
to some other kind of structure?

00:39:31.171 --> 00:39:34.060
&gt;&gt; There's a kind
of an illusion to

00:39:34.061 --> 00:39:38.780
something that Willard Van
Orman Quine wrote but,

00:39:38.781 --> 00:39:41.100
I don't know. Maybe that's it.

00:39:41.101 --> 00:39:43.640
The web of relations.

00:39:43.641 --> 00:39:47.260
I'm not sure how much
I've loaded into that,

00:39:47.261 --> 00:39:48.715
except that I think that,

00:39:48.716 --> 00:39:53.275
you have to think of
physical individuals

00:39:53.276 --> 00:39:56.680
as nodes in a network

00:39:56.681 --> 00:39:59.650
or a web and that
that's a significant.

00:39:59.651 --> 00:40:04.495
So both the connections and
the nodes are important.

00:40:04.496 --> 00:40:06.450
So, I don't know.

00:40:06.451 --> 00:40:08.725
That's probably not
a very good answer.

00:40:08.726 --> 00:40:12.110
I thought it was cool.
The picture was cool.

00:40:13.460 --> 00:40:18.015
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] biological or
some other kind of connective-.

00:40:18.016 --> 00:40:20.295
&gt;&gt; Yes. There will be
connective tissue.

00:40:20.296 --> 00:40:28.455
Network is the other
other metaphor.

00:40:28.456 --> 00:40:32.240
So, my conclusion the
normative web including

00:40:32.241 --> 00:40:33.845
linguistic norms that connect

00:40:33.846 --> 00:40:38.440
individuals who are capable
of moral personhood.

00:40:38.441 --> 00:40:41.460
And without which, there
could be no moral persons,

00:40:41.461 --> 00:40:43.970
it has to be itself
intrinsically valuable.

00:40:43.971 --> 00:40:46.310
So, it's not just the individuals

00:40:46.311 --> 00:40:47.780
but all of our connectedness.

00:40:47.781 --> 00:40:50.150
So, this is a relational accounts

00:40:50.151 --> 00:40:52.280
or our relations
with each other are

00:40:52.281 --> 00:40:55.460
also intrinsically valuable not

00:40:55.461 --> 00:40:59.025
just the individual node. Yes?

00:40:59.026 --> 00:41:01.770
&gt;&gt; I don't know, maybe somebody
ask something like that.

00:41:01.771 --> 00:41:05.490
Does it count if you have
a relationship with let's say,

00:41:05.491 --> 00:41:07.920
with the environment with nature.

00:41:07.921 --> 00:41:09.840
I'm thinking about
indigenous community

00:41:09.841 --> 00:41:11.730
and their sense of society.

00:41:11.731 --> 00:41:17.355
So, I am kind of such person
if a relate with the tree.

00:41:17.356 --> 00:41:22.530
For me a tree is a living being
that determine my norms.

00:41:22.531 --> 00:41:25.680
Because it's influencing
my behavior.

00:41:25.681 --> 00:41:29.820
So that the individual
can be something thus,

00:41:29.821 --> 00:41:31.440
not human. You know what I mean?

00:41:31.441 --> 00:41:32.970
&gt;&gt; So, the thing is,

00:41:32.971 --> 00:41:35.955
a tree can't be a
self-owner on my view.

00:41:35.956 --> 00:41:39.900
Okay? Doesn't mean that
the tree is invaluable.

00:41:39.901 --> 00:41:41.460
But on my view,

00:41:41.461 --> 00:41:43.545
it would have to be
instrumentally valuable.

00:41:43.546 --> 00:41:47.715
And so, I would have to
have a long conversation to

00:41:47.716 --> 00:41:50.430
figure out a way in
which I would be able to

00:41:50.431 --> 00:41:53.234
see the tree as
intrinsically valuable,

00:41:53.235 --> 00:41:55.455
in the way that self owners are.

00:41:55.456 --> 00:41:57.825
&gt;&gt; The man or the woman
that interact

00:41:57.826 --> 00:42:00.645
just with the tree that say
other people don't exist,

00:42:00.646 --> 00:42:03.210
intrinsically valuable or not?

00:42:03.211 --> 00:42:06.400
&gt;&gt; No, because it
can't be self owner.

00:42:06.590 --> 00:42:10.020
Right? Now I could imagine,

00:42:10.021 --> 00:42:12.300
that suppose there's one human

00:42:12.301 --> 00:42:14.650
being ever and there's also

00:42:14.651 --> 00:42:20.050
one other sentient being that can

00:42:20.051 --> 00:42:23.710
also form intentions where

00:42:23.711 --> 00:42:25.930
they could interact in some way.

00:42:25.931 --> 00:42:27.550
I could imagine that they could

00:42:27.551 --> 00:42:29.590
develop a kind of
language between

00:42:29.591 --> 00:42:33.530
them and eventually both
become self owners,

00:42:33.531 --> 00:42:35.440
that seems imaginable to me,

00:42:35.441 --> 00:42:38.695
like higher-order primates
or whales or dolphins.

00:42:38.696 --> 00:42:40.885
So, I think there are probably-.

00:42:40.886 --> 00:42:43.930
I'm not enough of a biologist
to know which things are.

00:42:43.931 --> 00:42:46.540
I'm pretty sure trees can't
be because they can't

00:42:46.541 --> 00:42:49.730
form intentions or
beliefs or desires. Yes?

00:42:49.731 --> 00:42:51.130
&gt;&gt; I'm really curious
because I feel

