00:43:27.631 --> 00:43:28.960
despite the fact
that we're actually

00:43:28.961 --> 00:43:30.300
connected and we all know that,

00:43:30.301 --> 00:43:32.050
there's a convenient fiction

00:43:32.051 --> 00:43:33.990
that's been there that
we kind of lean on.

00:43:33.991 --> 00:43:34.361
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:43:34.361 --> 00:43:36.760
&gt;&gt; You're trying to
just like take fiction.

00:43:36.761 --> 00:43:37.780
&gt;&gt; Right, yes.

00:43:37.781 --> 00:43:40.445
&gt;&gt; Okay. That's great. Then,
one questions is like,

00:43:40.446 --> 00:43:42.130
maybe it's a fiction and it is

00:43:42.131 --> 00:43:44.695
a convenient fiction and it
helps us organize things,

00:43:44.696 --> 00:43:47.350
and we could say it's
pragmatic fiction or we can say

00:43:47.351 --> 00:43:48.850
maybe there would be
a better fiction that

00:43:48.851 --> 00:43:50.470
would better understand this.

00:43:50.471 --> 00:43:53.110
But it makes sense

00:43:53.111 --> 00:43:55.660
to me that there's a world
where I both recognize

00:43:55.661 --> 00:43:56.920
that I am deeply tethered to

00:43:56.921 --> 00:43:58.330
people through
social relations in

00:43:58.331 --> 00:44:01.675
language but I still have
way to like have contracts,

00:44:01.676 --> 00:44:03.535
and that contracts
relies on like,

00:44:03.536 --> 00:44:05.230
I'm me, you're you.

00:44:05.231 --> 00:44:07.435
I have stuff. I can give you
stuff. That's your stuff.

00:44:07.436 --> 00:44:10.870
&gt;&gt; Great. Yes. Right.
So, that's exactly-.

00:44:10.871 --> 00:44:12.490
&gt;&gt; We don't have to be like.
No, I believe in atomistic

00:44:12.491 --> 00:44:14.960
individuals, should actually
get to like, is this

00:44:14.961 --> 00:44:17.330
a foolish fiction for us
to keep on sticking with?

00:44:17.331 --> 00:44:19.475
&gt;&gt; Yes. Yes. That's exactly

00:44:19.476 --> 00:44:23.315
the point that I want to get to.

00:44:23.316 --> 00:44:27.150
So, the response is often that,

00:44:27.151 --> 00:44:28.820
well, this is
just an idealization,

00:44:28.821 --> 00:44:30.740
this atomistic individual, just

00:44:30.741 --> 00:44:33.410
like idealizations in physics,

00:44:33.411 --> 00:44:35.675
in economics, whatever, right?

00:44:35.676 --> 00:44:39.030
And my response to
that is to say,

00:44:39.031 --> 00:44:42.555
the value of an ideal
In that sense,

00:44:42.556 --> 00:44:45.345
depends on its use, right?

00:44:45.346 --> 00:44:47.295
What does the theory give you?

00:44:47.296 --> 00:44:53.565
That you get out of
making that idealization.

00:44:53.566 --> 00:44:56.700
Well, in political philosophy,

00:44:56.701 --> 00:44:59.040
it gives you libetarianisim.

00:44:59.041 --> 00:45:03.090
So, it gives you a theory
that says you have

00:45:03.091 --> 00:45:06.210
no necessary obligations to

00:45:06.211 --> 00:45:09.675
others that you don't
voluntary contract for, okay?

00:45:09.676 --> 00:45:11.475
I think that's a real problem.

00:45:11.476 --> 00:45:13.365
I think that's a problem
for the world.

00:45:13.366 --> 00:45:15.210
I think that we actually owe each

00:45:15.211 --> 00:45:17.910
other some things by virtue

00:45:17.911 --> 00:45:20.160
of what we are given without ever

00:45:20.161 --> 00:45:23.220
asking or accepting voluntarily,

00:45:23.221 --> 00:45:28.215
those things, that we
inevitably have so much,

00:45:28.216 --> 00:45:30.855
that we owe it,

00:45:30.856 --> 00:45:35.040
to the pay it forward or pay
it back one or the other.

00:45:35.041 --> 00:45:37.170
We have an obligation to do that.

00:45:37.171 --> 00:45:40.170
And so, the libertarian ideal

00:45:40.171 --> 00:45:42.360
that you actually should have,

00:45:42.361 --> 00:45:44.310
the tax list state

00:45:44.311 --> 00:45:47.985
except for defense
something like that,

00:45:47.986 --> 00:45:51.300
is a mistake and it comes

00:45:51.301 --> 00:45:54.210
about as a result of
this ideal, idealization.

00:45:54.211 --> 00:45:57.840
So, I judge the value of
an ideal based on what you

00:45:57.841 --> 00:45:59.400
get out of it and I think you

00:45:59.401 --> 00:46:01.800
get something not
so good out of it.

00:46:01.801 --> 00:46:05.700
So, it's question begging to say,

00:46:05.701 --> 00:46:07.470
you know this, oh but it's just

00:46:07.471 --> 00:46:10.770
an ideal but it gives
me this bad outcomes.

00:46:10.771 --> 00:46:13.790
So let's examine the ideal.
The ideals of fiction.

00:46:13.791 --> 00:46:15.705
Here's the way in
which it's a fiction.

00:46:15.706 --> 00:46:17.130
What would this give us?

00:46:17.131 --> 00:46:21.870
Alright? Well, I'm going
to pass on by this one

00:46:21.871 --> 00:46:26.510
because that was just to
say there are lots of

00:46:26.511 --> 00:46:28.955
different causal origins
of different but

00:46:28.956 --> 00:46:31.980
let's move on to
Autonomy as ideal.

00:46:31.981 --> 00:46:35.000
So, if we are

00:46:35.001 --> 00:46:39.095
the self owners what is
an ideal way to be a self owner.

00:46:39.096 --> 00:46:40.805
Well traditionally,

00:46:40.806 --> 00:46:43.280
autonomy has been a value

00:46:43.281 --> 00:46:45.080
in moral and political philosophy

00:46:45.081 --> 00:46:47.390
for lots of reasons I could
talk about later but it's

00:46:47.391 --> 00:46:51.140
the basic idea of people
as self governors.

00:46:51.141 --> 00:46:53.480
So, it's an ideal of freedom.

00:46:53.481 --> 00:46:58.010
Right? I get to

00:46:58.011 --> 00:47:00.170
set my own beliefs and
desires and so forth,

00:47:00.171 --> 00:47:02.580
and nobody forces them upon me.

00:47:02.581 --> 00:47:08.570
Autonomy as long
been thought of as

00:47:08.571 --> 00:47:11.835
a kind of individualistic notion.

00:47:11.836 --> 00:47:13.820
But more recently,
and I'm talking

00:47:13.821 --> 00:47:15.440
about 20 years now or something

00:47:15.441 --> 00:47:17.600
of political and
moral philosophy,

00:47:17.601 --> 00:47:20.630
a notion of relational
autonomy has

00:47:20.631 --> 00:47:25.010
been developed and
particularly by feminists.

00:47:25.011 --> 00:47:29.390
And so, that's the ideal
of autonomy that would go

00:47:29.391 --> 00:47:33.470
together with this connected
self ideal, alright?

00:47:33.471 --> 00:47:36.730
So, one of these,

00:47:36.731 --> 00:47:39.660
that's particularly
appealing to me is

00:47:39.661 --> 00:47:42.090
the idea that
an autonomous person is

00:47:42.091 --> 00:47:44.760
one who has the ability

00:47:44.761 --> 00:47:47.530
to participate in
a collective self-government.

00:47:47.531 --> 00:47:51.800
So is not only a taker of
norms but also a giver.

00:47:51.801 --> 00:47:54.570
So, can give and take,

00:47:54.571 --> 00:47:58.184
argue about debate, disagree,

00:47:58.185 --> 00:48:00.830
agree, whatever about the norm.

00:48:00.831 --> 00:48:03.200
So, on my view,

00:48:03.201 --> 00:48:05.040
the connected self could not be

00:48:05.041 --> 00:48:07.545
a lone originator of norms,

00:48:07.546 --> 00:48:09.615
alright? Got to be others.

00:48:09.616 --> 00:48:12.910
But autonomous
connected selves are both

00:48:12.911 --> 00:48:14.740
contributors to and participants

00:48:14.741 --> 00:48:17.850
in the making and giving
of normative guidance.

00:48:20.190 --> 00:48:23.010
Now, we get a little
bit of pay off.

00:48:23.011 --> 00:48:24.870
There's not very much, because

00:48:24.871 --> 00:48:26.050
I don't have a lot of time.

00:48:26.051 --> 00:48:27.940
But a little bit of
payoff is, well,

00:48:27.941 --> 00:48:31.510
the web of connection society,
community, networks,

00:48:31.511 --> 00:48:34.630
ought to allow each node
within to become

00:48:34.631 --> 00:48:38.560
such a reciprocal participant
giver and taker of

00:48:38.561 --> 00:48:40.720
norms in order to

00:48:40.721 --> 00:48:43.580
promote this kind of
relational autonomy.

00:48:43.581 --> 00:48:45.860
So, we could now judge

00:48:45.861 --> 00:48:50.970
communities as oppressive
or as autonomy enhancing.

00:48:50.971 --> 00:48:53.259
So those would be two opposites,

00:48:53.260 --> 00:48:56.100
depending on whether they enhance

00:48:56.101 --> 00:48:57.360
people's ability to be

00:48:57.361 --> 00:49:00.480
full participants in
this norm creation,

00:49:00.481 --> 00:49:03.795
or whether for some people

00:49:03.796 --> 00:49:05.520
merely be takers and

00:49:05.521 --> 00:49:08.680
the others get to be
both givers and takers.

00:49:08.730 --> 00:49:11.580
Okay?

00:49:11.581 --> 00:49:13.740
So, this is just the
basic difference between

00:49:13.741 --> 00:49:16.990
the atomistic self-owners
and the connected.

00:49:20.930 --> 00:49:24.100
I am going to pass out and by too

00:49:24.101 --> 00:49:27.640
because that's
basically [inaudible].

00:49:27.641 --> 00:49:29.050
So, what are these

00:49:29.051 --> 00:49:31.350
autonomy enhancing
communities or networks?

00:49:31.351 --> 00:49:34.180
Well, they're the ones that
encourage reciprocity.

00:49:34.181 --> 00:49:35.500
And one way of doing that is by

00:49:35.501 --> 00:49:40.120
encouraging or having norms
of dignity and equality.

00:49:40.121 --> 00:49:44.800
Because if everybody has
dignity and equality,

00:49:44.801 --> 00:49:48.115
they're authorized by
the normative framework itself

00:49:48.116 --> 00:49:52.710
to be givers as well
as takers of norms.

00:49:52.711 --> 00:49:55.080
So, now I'm getting
a little bit of

00:49:55.081 --> 00:49:57.705
political payoff here to this.

00:49:57.706 --> 00:50:02.190
And morally speaking,
connected selves,

00:50:02.191 --> 00:50:04.500
good connected selves,
moral ones,

00:50:04.501 --> 00:50:06.945
nourish autonomy in each other

00:50:06.946 --> 00:50:10.465
by teaching and encouraging
autonomous capacities,

00:50:10.466 --> 00:50:13.199
that is capacities
to debate civilly

00:50:13.200 --> 00:50:18.030
or without violence and

00:50:18.031 --> 00:50:20.675
to talk about norms
with each other,

00:50:20.676 --> 00:50:22.660
which, of course,
we do all the time.

00:50:22.661 --> 00:50:26.670
This is what much of
our conversation is about.

00:50:26.671 --> 00:50:29.320
It's about normative facts.

00:50:29.990 --> 00:50:32.740
And by discouraging violence and

00:50:32.741 --> 00:50:34.360
other emotionally or socially

00:50:34.361 --> 00:50:36.370
or psychologically
damaging practices,

00:50:36.371 --> 00:50:39.370
which disable us from being

00:50:39.371 --> 00:50:40.870
these full participants in

00:50:40.871 --> 00:50:43.985
the reciprocity of norm
giving and taking.

00:50:43.986 --> 00:50:47.860
And so, basically, this
is a basic principle I

00:50:47.861 --> 00:50:49.780
think and that is
networks or communities

00:50:49.781 --> 00:50:51.910
that are not autonomy enhancing,

00:50:51.911 --> 00:50:54.490
have no right to exist as such,

00:50:54.491 --> 00:50:57.340
and persons have
moral obligations to reform

00:50:57.341 --> 00:50:59.860
those or to help those who are

00:50:59.861 --> 00:51:02.410
oppressed by them to find

00:51:02.411 --> 00:51:07.630
autonomy enhancing networks
in which two exist.

00:51:07.631 --> 00:51:11.070
So, now we can talk a little bit

00:51:11.071 --> 00:51:14.820
about online communities and,

00:51:14.821 --> 00:51:23.380
the sort of reciprocity that
should be enabled by them.

00:51:23.381 --> 00:51:26.585
So, just like communities
in the material world,

00:51:26.586 --> 00:51:28.260
online communities can be either

00:51:28.261 --> 00:51:30.270
oppressive or autonomy enhancing.

00:51:30.271 --> 00:51:31.710
So, if there are norms of

00:51:31.711 --> 00:51:35.125
bullying and shutting
people down and so forth,

00:51:35.126 --> 00:51:38.240
those are obviously
non-autonomy enhancing.

00:51:38.241 --> 00:51:45.175
There are some networks,

00:51:45.176 --> 00:51:49.345
some online sharing spaces

00:51:49.346 --> 00:51:55.175
that provide the ability
to have full conversations

00:51:55.176 --> 00:52:02.590
without flaming and that
sort of thing but allow for

00:52:02.591 --> 00:52:07.420
continuing threaded
discussions in ways that

00:52:07.421 --> 00:52:13.580
are more productive or to say.

00:52:14.250 --> 00:52:18.000
And obviously, spreading
false news is one way

00:52:18.001 --> 00:52:21.470
of psychologically
damaging people.

00:52:21.471 --> 00:52:23.930
And then just for privacy rights,

00:52:23.931 --> 00:52:32.135
I think we have a duty
not to harm others,

00:52:32.136 --> 00:52:35.905
that's not autonomy-enhancing
after all.

00:52:35.906 --> 00:52:39.240
And norms of trolling
behavior or creating

00:52:39.241 --> 00:52:42.479
false news that gaslights persons

00:52:42.480 --> 00:52:45.900
or making defamatory false claims

00:52:45.901 --> 00:52:48.810
about people and those
kinds of things,

00:52:48.811 --> 00:52:51.330
those are kinds of
privacy violations

00:52:51.331 --> 00:52:54.780
and some of them,
they cause harm.

00:52:54.781 --> 00:52:57.670
We have a duty I think to

00:52:57.671 --> 00:53:01.390
avoid doing things like
harvesting personal data

00:53:01.391 --> 00:53:03.190
without permission or passing on

00:53:03.191 --> 00:53:05.620
data that's been
legitimately collected in

00:53:05.621 --> 00:53:07.915
one way but using in ways

00:53:07.916 --> 00:53:10.570
that then lead to
these kinds of harms or

00:53:10.571 --> 00:53:18.055
that can lead to non-autonomy
enhancing networks.

00:53:18.056 --> 00:53:22.180
Right? And so, I would say we

00:53:22.181 --> 00:53:26.260
can derive the idea that
software developers have

00:53:26.261 --> 00:53:28.930
a duty to avoid having
their products be

00:53:28.931 --> 00:53:32.575
used in such a way
that they harvest

00:53:32.576 --> 00:53:34.780
personal data without
permission or

00:53:34.781 --> 00:53:37.360
pass on data that's
been legitimately

00:53:37.361 --> 00:53:43.600
collected in various ways. Yes?

00:53:43.601 --> 00:53:46.600
&gt;&gt; What do you think about
different cultures having

00:53:46.601 --> 00:53:49.870
different normative
webs, for example,

00:53:49.871 --> 00:53:50.950
the United States and

00:53:50.951 --> 00:53:54.715
the European Union having
different ideals about

00:53:54.716 --> 00:53:58.510
self-control and
autonomy and that we're

00:53:58.511 --> 00:54:01.780
seeing in exact this issue?

00:54:01.781 --> 00:54:02.620
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:54:02.621 --> 00:54:04.150
&gt;&gt; Now Facebook is
just like, well,

00:54:04.151 --> 00:54:05.885
we're not quite gonna
give North Americans

00:54:05.886 --> 00:54:08.875
the same kind of privacy
protection as we give Europeans.

00:54:08.876 --> 00:54:11.670
&gt;&gt; No. That's just because
they want to abide

00:54:11.671 --> 00:54:14.660
by the minimal laws rather than-.

00:54:14.661 --> 00:54:16.270
&gt;&gt; Right. They want to
abide by minimal laws?

00:54:16.271 --> 00:54:17.845
Is that the case where,

00:54:17.846 --> 00:54:20.515
from your perspective,
you can say, well,

00:54:20.516 --> 00:54:24.160
Facebook is infringing on

00:54:24.161 --> 00:54:28.040
the autonomy of
Americans and Canadians.

00:54:28.140 --> 00:54:34.130
They're wrong or that's not
more moral way behave or is

00:54:34.131 --> 00:54:36.670
there an argument that could
be legitimately made but

00:54:36.671 --> 00:54:39.330
instead we're abiding by
the norms of our community,

00:54:39.331 --> 00:54:41.880
which is the American
community and why should

00:54:41.881 --> 00:54:44.460
we be cultured by the norms
of another communities?

00:54:44.461 --> 00:54:46.920
&gt;&gt; Good. So, what
my theory allows us to do

00:54:46.921 --> 00:54:49.875
is to judge entire
normative frameworks.

00:54:49.876 --> 00:54:52.080
Right? So, what we
could say is that

00:54:52.081 --> 00:54:54.570
it turns out the North
American normative framework,

00:54:54.571 --> 00:54:57.630
legal framework is not
the best normative framework.

00:54:57.631 --> 00:54:59.980
The EU has a better one, right?

00:54:59.981 --> 00:55:02.310
I mean, you could make it in

00:55:02.311 --> 00:55:04.965
terms of this
autonomy enhancement,

00:55:04.966 --> 00:55:09.080
and the likelihood of
creating the False-light,

00:55:09.081 --> 00:55:13.610
false news and harvesting
personal data.

00:55:13.611 --> 00:55:15.570
I didn't say much
about why that's

00:55:15.571 --> 00:55:18.090
so bad violations of privacy,

00:55:18.091 --> 00:55:22.620
but I could talk some
more about that.

00:55:22.950 --> 00:55:26.250
So, this I think is

00:55:26.251 --> 00:55:27.630
a framework that gives us

00:55:27.631 --> 00:55:29.550
exactly the kind of ammunition we

00:55:29.551 --> 00:55:32.400
need to argue that
the whole framework of

00:55:32.401 --> 00:55:36.165
the legal structure is
inadequate at this point.

00:55:36.166 --> 00:55:40.290
Right? Not that legal structures
are the only ways

00:55:40.291 --> 00:55:41.895
of creating normative frameworks

00:55:41.896 --> 00:55:44.430
but they're pretty important
and it seems like,

00:55:44.431 --> 00:55:48.480
if we have a sharp
disagreements like

00:55:48.481 --> 00:55:53.310
Facebook on one side and
others on the other,

00:55:53.311 --> 00:55:55.670
we're going to have
to adjudicate that in

00:55:55.671 --> 00:55:57.750
a way probably that uses
a certain amount of

00:55:57.751 --> 00:56:00.435
coercion and legal
forces probably

00:56:00.436 --> 00:56:04.260
what's required. Yes Bill?

