But I should also say
that I think lots of

00:57:27.491 --> 00:57:31.020
autonomy-enhancing normative
frameworks and so,

00:57:31.021 --> 00:57:34.080
you could have two different
communities that have

00:57:34.081 --> 00:57:38.020
very different norms, well,

00:57:38.021 --> 00:57:42.165
especially aesthetic
norms, etiquette norms,

00:57:42.166 --> 00:57:45.570
moral norms, still there

00:57:45.571 --> 00:57:47.760
could be some differences
in moral norms I think.

00:57:47.761 --> 00:57:55.050
That could both be
autonomy-enhancing and yet,

00:57:55.051 --> 00:57:56.730
not exactly the same.

00:57:56.731 --> 00:58:00.765
Okay? So, when they
come into conflict,

00:58:00.766 --> 00:58:04.290
probably some respect norms
that need to come into

00:58:04.291 --> 00:58:10.805
play in order to enhance
the autonomy of both.

00:58:10.806 --> 00:58:13.125
But I do think that

00:58:13.126 --> 00:58:18.000
individuals need to have the
right to abandon one set

00:58:18.001 --> 00:58:22.580
of norms and go to
another community

00:58:22.581 --> 00:58:25.340
if their autonomy is not

00:58:25.341 --> 00:58:28.615
enhanced within
a particular community.

00:58:28.616 --> 00:58:32.110
So, once a self-owner,

00:58:32.111 --> 00:58:34.055
always a self-owner, sort of.

00:58:34.056 --> 00:58:36.400
So, you don't have to speak
the language that you're

00:58:36.401 --> 00:58:37.880
dropped into a new country

00:58:37.881 --> 00:58:39.160
and you don't speak
their language,

00:58:39.161 --> 00:58:40.955
you're still a self-owner.

00:58:40.956 --> 00:58:43.830
But you're going to
have to probably to be

00:58:43.831 --> 00:58:47.115
an autonomous self-owner
within this new community,

00:58:47.116 --> 00:58:48.450
you're going to have
to learn some of

00:58:48.451 --> 00:58:49.800
their language in order to be

00:58:49.801 --> 00:58:53.295
able to participate. Yes?

00:58:53.296 --> 00:58:54.700
&gt;&gt; So,

00:58:54.701 --> 00:58:59.315
continuing this questioning
of normative frameworks,

00:58:59.316 --> 00:59:03.540
it seems we're still trapped

00:59:03.541 --> 00:59:04.890
a little bit in

00:59:04.891 --> 00:59:07.030
this libertarian
framework because

00:59:07.031 --> 00:59:09.565
emphasis on privacy rights.

00:59:09.566 --> 00:59:11.020
I think this pervades

00:59:11.021 --> 00:59:12.800
a lot of current
policies of course,

00:59:12.801 --> 00:59:14.910
if we just can't protect

00:59:14.911 --> 00:59:18.745
our privacy or if we are
able to own our own data.

00:59:18.746 --> 00:59:20.945
So, there's still this adoption

00:59:20.946 --> 00:59:23.080
of a kind of
individualistic framework,

00:59:23.081 --> 00:59:25.950
and I'm just wondering if
we can broad in the social

00:59:25.951 --> 00:59:27.675
imaginary and think
about the trend

00:59:27.676 --> 00:59:29.985
of not just individuals,

00:59:29.986 --> 00:59:33.975
but social rights,
collective rights.

00:59:33.976 --> 00:59:37.255
Is that deserve a vocabulary
we can move from?

00:59:37.256 --> 00:59:39.260
&gt;&gt; Well, one thing is I
think it's important,

00:59:39.261 --> 00:59:40.850
I don't know, if this gets there,

00:59:40.851 --> 00:59:42.340
but it's really
important to see why

00:59:42.341 --> 00:59:46.510
publicity is so important
to us as well as privacy.

00:59:46.511 --> 00:59:51.475
So, that's a value because
without publicity,

00:59:51.476 --> 00:59:54.880
our ability to make public to

00:59:54.881 --> 00:59:59.460
each other what we think
and what we believe,

00:59:59.461 --> 01:00:02.455
we don't have a normative web.

01:00:02.456 --> 01:00:08.090
So, publicity is very
valuable as is privacy,

01:00:08.091 --> 01:00:12.410
and I think privacy is
valuable because, again,

01:00:12.411 --> 01:00:15.120
each of us is a node,

01:00:15.121 --> 01:00:20.130
each of us does suffer
an individual fate,

01:00:20.131 --> 01:00:24.090
if you will, and can
be individually picked

01:00:24.091 --> 01:00:28.930
out for abuse or
something like that.

01:00:28.931 --> 01:00:34.950
So, what I think the connected
self-ownership ideal

01:00:34.951 --> 01:00:37.540
helps us to see is how
important it is to

01:00:37.541 --> 01:00:41.595
balance the needs of publicity
and the needs of privacy.

01:00:41.596 --> 01:00:44.150
But maybe you're right,
maybe that's still doesn't

01:00:44.151 --> 01:00:47.735
get beyond the individualism.

01:00:47.736 --> 01:00:51.000
As I said, I am ultimately
a liberal just not

01:00:51.001 --> 01:00:54.550
a libertarian and
normative individualism

01:00:54.551 --> 01:00:55.870
is something I embrace.

01:00:55.871 --> 01:00:57.420
So, maybe I can get

01:00:57.421 --> 01:01:00.290
totally over to
the communitarian side.

01:01:00.291 --> 01:01:02.340
So, let's see. Yes.

01:01:02.341 --> 01:01:06.915
&gt;&gt; So, as much as I
like this idea of,

01:01:06.916 --> 01:01:09.775
this connecting to self-owner,

01:01:09.776 --> 01:01:13.145
I'm seeing several
problems if you try

01:01:13.146 --> 01:01:17.265
to put it out there as
a strategy on the Internet.

01:01:17.266 --> 01:01:22.385
So, one is that a lot of
these terms you're using,

01:01:22.386 --> 01:01:25.480
which I, again, that I love,

01:01:25.481 --> 01:01:30.685
like normative web and social
relations and all this,

01:01:30.686 --> 01:01:32.470
a libertarian is
just not going to

01:01:32.471 --> 01:01:35.270
listen to any of those concepts.

01:01:37.230 --> 01:01:40.610
Secondly, one thing that
we've learned about

01:01:40.611 --> 01:01:43.950
libertarians or similar behavior,

01:01:43.951 --> 01:01:46.445
especially in the last
lecture, if I recall,

01:01:46.446 --> 01:01:49.110
from sociologists, is that

01:01:49.111 --> 01:01:53.715
their notion of group is
very different from what,

01:01:53.716 --> 01:01:56.610
say, me as a sociologist
would consider a group.

01:01:56.611 --> 01:01:58.890
So, like Ally showed
us had this book

01:01:58.891 --> 01:02:03.380
on Tea Party Movement.

01:02:03.381 --> 01:02:06.690
The way that this group thinks

01:02:06.691 --> 01:02:09.910
about who's their inner circle
is very different.

01:02:09.911 --> 01:02:11.555
It's not everybody's,
it's not the entire web,

01:02:11.556 --> 01:02:14.165
and there are
various groups on the web.

01:02:14.166 --> 01:02:17.240
It's their immediate circle.

01:02:17.241 --> 01:02:19.685
That's the care group
that they would

01:02:19.686 --> 01:02:22.285
care about in a quorum forum.

01:02:22.286 --> 01:02:24.420
So, how would you make

01:02:24.421 --> 01:02:26.940
them think about
the web is normally

01:02:26.941 --> 01:02:33.510
web is this larger community.

01:02:33.740 --> 01:02:35.560
Third, who's
the subject that we're

01:02:35.561 --> 01:02:36.990
really talking about here?

01:02:36.991 --> 01:02:39.500
So, we have all these
individuals that are on the web

01:02:39.501 --> 01:02:43.560
and many of them are thinking,

01:02:43.561 --> 01:02:46.230
"I have libertarian rights,
I can do what I want.

01:02:46.231 --> 01:02:47.775
I can hate speech,

01:02:47.776 --> 01:02:52.220
I can do, this is
sort of my right."

01:02:52.221 --> 01:02:55.285
But it's not just
individuals now we know.

01:02:55.286 --> 01:02:59.420
The difficulty thing is
that the trolling and

01:02:59.421 --> 01:03:02.500
the false news is now

01:03:02.501 --> 01:03:06.130
being conducted by
one, bots, right?

01:03:06.131 --> 01:03:06.596
&gt;&gt; Right.

01:03:06.596 --> 01:03:08.735
&gt;&gt; You know, we can't
really talk to.

01:03:08.736 --> 01:03:14.245
Number two, by this Cambridge
Analytica and Russian,

01:03:14.246 --> 01:03:18.115
what do you call them?

01:03:18.116 --> 01:03:21.155
The trolling
organizations and such.

01:03:21.156 --> 01:03:26.975
So, it's these
elicit organizations

01:03:26.976 --> 01:03:29.520
and it's these tech designers,

01:03:29.521 --> 01:03:34.470
and now it's like
Zuckerberg who is, himself,

01:03:34.471 --> 01:03:38.750
like aligned with these groups

01:03:38.751 --> 01:03:40.280
who are doing these things.

01:03:40.281 --> 01:03:43.660
So, this idea that
there's a single sort of

01:03:43.661 --> 01:03:46.780
libertarian person that we

01:03:46.781 --> 01:03:48.545
have to convince,
it's so difficult.

01:03:48.546 --> 01:03:50.460
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, philosophy

01:03:50.461 --> 01:03:53.360
is pretty impotent,
there's no doubt about it.

01:03:53.430 --> 01:03:59.655
That is, what I can say
is you libertarian,

01:03:59.656 --> 01:04:02.530
you have the wrong
metaphysics itself.

01:04:02.610 --> 01:04:05.590
That's not very powerful in

01:04:05.591 --> 01:04:08.210
the world of coercive agents who

01:04:08.211 --> 01:04:13.135
are willing to use force to
get what they want. I agree.

01:04:13.136 --> 01:04:15.110
But those who say, "Well,

01:04:15.111 --> 01:04:20.035
but I've got these rights
and I-" That's actually no,

01:04:20.036 --> 01:04:24.545
if that is supposed to
have some moral grounding,

01:04:24.546 --> 01:04:26.470
or some rational grounding,

01:04:26.471 --> 01:04:28.135
turns out you're wrong.

01:04:28.136 --> 01:04:31.160
But if you want to
coerce me, well,

01:04:31.161 --> 01:04:33.520
I'm going to have to
bring force against

01:04:33.521 --> 01:04:36.340
your force, that's true.

01:04:36.341 --> 01:04:39.900
So, philosophy is
ultimately impotent

01:04:39.901 --> 01:04:41.110
against people who are willing to

01:04:41.111 --> 01:04:43.675
use force against others.

01:04:43.676 --> 01:04:45.435
As for the bots though,

01:04:45.436 --> 01:04:48.790
they're created by actual
human beings and I'm saying,

01:04:48.791 --> 01:04:51.890
no, you have a duty not
to create these things.

01:04:51.891 --> 01:04:54.270
Having a duty,

01:04:56.090 --> 01:04:59.160
I am going to shun you if you

01:04:59.161 --> 01:05:02.360
don't stop creating those bots.

01:05:02.361 --> 01:05:06.590
That's not a very
powerful position

01:05:06.591 --> 01:05:09.575
in the world of bad people.

01:05:09.576 --> 01:05:15.765
But in the world of those
who are saying the right to,

01:05:15.766 --> 01:05:17.920
say, free speech regardless of

01:05:17.921 --> 01:05:22.080
the consequences for
the community, well, no.

01:05:22.081 --> 01:05:24.905
Actually, I'm going to
argue that you don't have

01:05:24.906 --> 01:05:29.285
this kind of right that's
completely unbounded.

01:05:29.286 --> 01:05:33.315
Yeah, so it depends
on the amount of

01:05:33.316 --> 01:05:37.775
force that your opponent's
willing to apply. Yeah.

01:05:37.776 --> 01:05:40.310
&gt;&gt; I'm interested in

01:05:40.311 --> 01:05:44.860
the ownership concept and
the foundation of it.

01:05:44.861 --> 01:05:48.370
I can see why it's a very
powerful anchor or grounding,

01:05:48.371 --> 01:05:50.875
but if there are

01:05:50.876 --> 01:05:53.290
elements implicit in

01:05:53.291 --> 01:05:55.850
ownership concept
dissociation with possession,

01:05:55.851 --> 01:05:59.770
with control, with domination.

01:06:00.540 --> 01:06:03.300
That sort of pull against

01:06:03.301 --> 01:06:06.015
what you're trying to do
on a connective side,

01:06:06.016 --> 01:06:09.340
and if there were
other groundings or metaphors,

01:06:09.341 --> 01:06:13.840
if we consider to do
this foundational working.

01:06:13.841 --> 01:06:16.590
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, maybe
too much disclosure.

01:06:16.591 --> 01:06:19.730
I was assigned to

01:06:19.731 --> 01:06:21.580
do some kind of critique
of libertarianism.

01:06:21.581 --> 01:06:23.300
So I took up
the self-ownership idea,

01:06:23.301 --> 01:06:24.215
and then I said, "Oh,

01:06:24.216 --> 01:06:27.070
there might be a different way
of thinking about it."

01:06:27.071 --> 01:06:28.480
But then the question is why

01:06:28.481 --> 01:06:31.350
preserve the idea
of self-ownership?

01:06:31.351 --> 01:06:33.980
I can think of five reasons.

01:06:33.981 --> 01:06:39.445
So, it retains
this idea of claim.

01:06:39.446 --> 01:06:42.475
So, ownership brings
with it claim.

01:06:42.476 --> 01:06:45.775
It preserves the idea
that there is

01:06:45.776 --> 01:06:47.405
this node in the self

01:06:47.406 --> 01:06:49.575
that is of primary
moral importance,

01:06:49.576 --> 01:06:50.610
the individualism,

01:06:50.611 --> 01:06:53.760
the normative individualism
I think comes with it.

01:06:53.840 --> 01:06:57.990
But it also, because it's
connected self-ownership,

01:06:57.991 --> 01:06:59.780
it suggests that the creation of

01:06:59.781 --> 01:07:01.945
selves is a collective endeavor.

01:07:01.946 --> 01:07:06.325
I think the ownership metaphor
brings some good things,

01:07:06.326 --> 01:07:08.465
but I agree, there are
some bad things too.

01:07:08.466 --> 01:07:10.450
But it's just investment,

01:07:10.451 --> 01:07:12.450
and that we're still
paying forward

01:07:12.451 --> 01:07:14.975
that's what investment is.

01:07:14.976 --> 01:07:16.680
That we have an obligation to

01:07:16.681 --> 01:07:18.400
invest ourselves
in the project of

01:07:18.401 --> 01:07:20.810
this norm creation in order to be

01:07:20.811 --> 01:07:24.205
really autonomous,
fully fulfilled beings.

01:07:24.206 --> 01:07:29.870
Then finally, it's
traditional liberal way.

01:07:29.871 --> 01:07:31.130
So, going back really,

01:07:31.131 --> 01:07:33.765
you can trace it back to
at least the 15th century,

01:07:33.766 --> 01:07:36.340
a traditional way of
thinking about it.

01:07:36.341 --> 01:07:39.290
Also we have a compliment
to somebody is to say,

01:07:39.291 --> 01:07:42.160
"Oh, you're very self possessed."

01:07:42.290 --> 01:07:45.175
That's a kind of
confidence and so forth

01:07:45.176 --> 01:07:48.485
that is supposedly admirable.

01:07:48.486 --> 01:07:50.530
So, there are
some good aspects to it,

01:07:50.531 --> 01:07:53.510
but I admit it might come up with

01:07:53.511 --> 01:07:59.085
so much baggage that it's
not a good idea. Yes.

01:07:59.086 --> 01:08:05.755
&gt;&gt; I wonder if played
with the idea of agency.

01:08:05.756 --> 01:08:08.800
I can see all the arguments
there and that makes sense.

01:08:08.801 --> 01:08:12.090
But within philosophy,
is there room to make

01:08:12.091 --> 01:08:17.080
agency the shadow
that's connective and

01:08:17.081 --> 01:08:20.030
still allows for
sense self-determination

01:08:20.031 --> 01:08:22.050
but the self can be

01:08:22.190 --> 01:08:25.940
indefinite or open-ended in

01:08:25.960 --> 01:08:30.240
a more, in an
indigenous approach?

01:08:30.241 --> 01:08:32.785
&gt;&gt; Yeah, absolutely.
The idea of agency doesn't

01:08:32.786 --> 01:08:35.840
necessarily come with
this idea of a claim,

01:08:35.841 --> 01:08:39.390
a self-possession claim,
but certainly

01:08:39.391 --> 01:08:41.685
agencies always a very
important concept

01:08:41.686 --> 01:08:44.260
for philosophers. So, yeah.

01:08:44.261 --> 01:08:46.300
&gt;&gt; I think that we're-

01:08:46.301 --> 01:08:46.930
&gt;&gt; We're a little over.

01:08:46.931 --> 01:08:48.425
&gt;&gt; We are past time.

01:08:48.426 --> 01:08:51.150
There are supposed
to be snacks we can

01:08:51.151 --> 01:08:55.155
mingle and chat some
more. Thank you so much.

01:08:55.156 --> 01:08:57.680
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much. Okay.

